 handle gracefully.\n\nThe output must be a JSON string inside implementation key. So we need to write Python code that defines solve function.\n\nAlso note: The instruction says \"Return only JSON-serializable values with the exact named fields. Raise ValueError for an infeasible or structurally invalid case instead of silently repairing it.\" So we must validate fields exist? The case will have the correct structure per fixture, but we can assume. However, we can check for missing keys if needed. I'll assume valid.\n\nNow produce implementation string.\n\nWe must output exactly one JSON object with key \"implementation\" and value being a string of Python source code. The entire response must be that JSON object, no markdown. So we'll construct a JSON object string with escaped quotes and newlines.\n\nLet me write the Python code:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    segments = case['segments']\n    budget = case['budget']\n    n = len(segments)\n    # Validate\n    total_min = sum(seg['minimum'] for seg in segments)\n    total_cap = sum(seg['capacity'] for seg in segments)\n    if budget < total_min:\n        raise ValueError(\"Budget below total minimum quota\")\n    if budget > total_cap:\n        raise ValueError(\"Budget exceeds total capacity\")\n    allocations = [seg['minimum'] for seg in segments]\n    remaining = budget - total_min\n    # While remaining > 0, allocate to highest score\n    while remaining > 0:\n        best_idx = -1\n        best_score = -1.0\n        for i, seg in enumerate(segments):\n            if allocations[i] < seg['capacity']:\n                score = seg['importance'] / (allocations[i] + 1)\n                if score > best_score:\n                    best_score = score\n                    best_idx = i\n                # If equal, earlier index already has best_idx (since we iterate in order, we don't update if equal)\n        if best_idx == -1:\n            # Should not happen because budget <= total_cap ensures capacity left\n            raise ValueError(\"Unable to allocate remaining budget; capacities exhausted\")\n        allocations[best_idx] += 1\n        remaining -= 1\n    by_name = {segments[i]['name']: allocations[i] for i in range(n)}\n    total = sum(allocations)\n    return {\n        \"allocations\": allocations,\n        \"by_name\": by_name,\n        \"total\": total\n    }\n\nBut note: The schema says by_name has dynamic keys from case.segments[*].name. So our dict is fine.\n\nPotential floating point issues: using float division; importance and positions are integers. That's fine.\n\nNow we need to output this as a JSON string inside implementation key. We'll escape newlines and backslashes and quotes.\n\nI'll construct the JSON manually: {\"implementation\":\"def solve(case):\\n    segments = case['segments']\\n    budget = case['budget']\\n    ...\\n    return ...\"}\n\nBut careful: The code contains single quotes and double quotes? Actually the code uses single quotes for dictionary keys. That is fine. We need to escape backslashes and double quotes within the string. The string itself is enclosed in double quotes. So we need to escape any double quote characters inside the code. Our code does not contain double quotes (all single quotes) so no need. Newlines should be escaped as \\n. Backslashes? None except in string literals? No.\n\nWe should also ensure there are no trailing spaces. Let me produce a compact version.\n\nAlso, the candidate_artifact mentions \"coarse_budget_controller\" but we just implement solve.\n\nNow output the JSON."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":1499,"completion_tokens":1620,"total_tokens":3119,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":1408},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":1306},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":1408,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":91},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}